Below is a concise outline of how one might systematically apply a DPLL-style procedure (with backtracking) to a 3-CNF formula:

1) Parse all clauses.
2) Perform initial simplifications:
   • Check for any unit clauses (none appear here).  
   • Check for any pure literals (none easily apparent).  
3) Pick a literal (for example, x1) and branch on x1 = True / x1 = False.  
4) After each choice, propagate consequences (e.g., removing satisfied clauses, simplifying clauses containing the negation of that literal, etc.).  
5) If a contradiction (empty clause) appears, backtrack and flip the choice.  
6) Continue this process, branching on new literals each time you have no forced assignments, and backtracking upon conflict.  

By exhaustively and systematically applying this procedure, one finds a satisfying assignment. The statistics below (branches and conflicts) illustrate the count of literal-choices made (branches) and the number of times a contradiction forced a backtrack (conflicts).

